From f1ee0d7ec37fdd1e247b059fdf4e55b18b6cbf79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 12:52:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Count the free half of item 3-1's read side from the tree's
 leaf save

The whole read side is not obtainable: wrapping a local's read
expression crashes the compiler, because variable.Expression is also the
assignment target, so x++ becomes a store onto a method call. The
guarded tree's leaf save is the one read position with neither problem
-- the value is the right-hand side of an assignment into a fresh
temporary, never a target, and BuildOrderedTree runs only after every
refusal has been decided on the syntax, so a counter there can change
neither what compiles nor which trees specialize.

Both claims are checked rather than argued. Re-running the census with
the counter on reproduces the roots-consumed-by-a-refused-local count
exactly -- 18 657 518 against 18 657 815, 1.000 on every suite
individually -- and the three suites that fail under it (RegExp,
Mandreel, zlib) fail identically with it off.

Of 16 426 708 boxed writes, the free leaf reads are 34 645 485, 2.11 per
write. By refusal: Parameter 12.89, CallResult 13.24, ElementRead 2.14,
PropertyRead 0.90, DroppedCandidate 0.72, NeverOffered 0.51. Gameboy's
ElementRead locals read 31.06 times per write and Box2D's 0.05, so the
shape is a property of the workload rather than of the cause.

This does not decide the item and is quoted as what it is. The break-
even condition is the BOXING reads, which have no safe hook, and item
3-8a is the standing warning against reading a large free-read count as
encouraging: it lost with 393 705 boxes minted at the read against
about 5 300 removed, and no count of the reads it served for free would
have predicted that.

3 further cases, one of which is the non-perturbation check as a test
rather than as a census comparison. 8 169 tests across eight projects,
no failures.
